WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.656
This file is about twelve times bigger than the memory we'll let our programs use.

00:00:05.906 --> 00:00:13.503
Ask a simple Python script to read every line into a list and sort it, and it runs out of memory just reading the file in.

00:00:13.753 --> 00:00:17.963
Ask the Unix sort command, with the same memory, and it just finishes.

00:00:18.213 --> 00:00:22.798
While it works, twelve files appear in a temporary folder, and then they vanish.

00:00:23.048 --> 00:00:25.847
What are those files, and how does sort get away with it?

00:00:29.597 --> 00:00:40.908
The obvious fix is virtual memory: treat the disk as memory, and let the operating system keep the parts it used recently in memory, fetching the rest from disk as needed.

00:00:41.158 --> 00:00:43.466
But a disk never fetches one number.

00:00:43.516 --> 00:00:53.527
It fetches a whole block, thousands of bytes at least, and every fetch takes hundreds to thousands of times longer than reading memory, even on a fast SSD.

00:00:53.777 --> 00:00:57.462
Moving one block between disk and memory is called an I/O.

00:00:57.512 --> 00:01:02.877
Once data doesn't fit in memory, the number of I/Os typically dominates the running time.

00:01:03.127 --> 00:01:06.252
Heapsort and merge sort are both N log N sorts.

00:01:06.302 --> 00:01:10.452
By comparisons alone, heapsort would be at most about twice as slow.

00:01:10.702 --> 00:01:14.285
But heapsort compares items that sit far apart in its array.

00:01:14.335 --> 00:01:21.439
Under virtual memory that array lives on disk, block by block, so heapsort keeps needing blocks that aren't in memory.

00:01:21.489 --> 00:01:29.457
Merge sort reads its input from front to back, and writes its output from front to back, so every block it fetches gets used completely.

00:01:29.707 --> 00:01:34.353
We simulated both under virtual memory on a quarter of a million items.

00:01:34.403 --> 00:01:38.611
Memory held a twelfth of them, roughly the proportions of sort's file.

00:01:38.661 --> 00:01:47.977
Heapsort made about twice as many comparisons as merge sort, but more than twenty times as many I/Os: about three for every item it sorts.

00:01:49.227 --> 00:01:56.112
Even if every comparison cost as much as a memory access, heapsort's comparisons would take under a second.

00:01:56.162 --> 00:01:58.063
Its I/Os take more than a minute.

00:01:58.113 --> 00:01:59.577
The I/Os decide.

00:02:01.027 --> 00:02:03.199
So merge sort is our starting point.

00:02:03.249 --> 00:02:11.747
It sweeps through the whole file again and again, so let's measure it in passes, where one pass reads and writes every block of the file once.

00:02:12.697 --> 00:02:19.276
Merge sort merges single items into pairs, then pairs into fours, doubling the pieces every pass.

00:02:19.326 --> 00:02:24.903
Eighteen doublings take you from one item to a quarter of a million, so that's eighteen passes.

00:02:25.153 --> 00:02:27.112
But watch the size of the pieces.

00:02:27.162 --> 00:02:31.147
For the first fourteen passes, every piece is smaller than memory.

00:02:31.197 --> 00:02:33.512
Merging only needs sorted pieces.

00:02:33.562 --> 00:02:39.485
It doesn't matter how they got sorted, so each of those pieces could have been sorted without touching the disk.

00:02:39.735 --> 00:02:41.303
So do exactly that.

00:02:41.353 --> 00:02:48.424
Fill memory with the start of the file, sort it right there, and write it out to disk as one sorted piece, called a run.

00:02:48.474 --> 00:02:51.795
Then do the same with the next part of the file, and the next.

00:02:52.045 --> 00:02:58.480
Our simulated file is exactly twelve memories long, so this makes twelve runs, in a single pass.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:03.825
That one pass covers everything the first fourteen did, so only the last four remain.

00:03:03.875 --> 00:03:05.793
Eighteen passes become five.

00:03:07.043 --> 00:03:14.159
And that's what sort's twelve files were: one run for each memory-full of its file, the last one only partly full.

00:03:15.109 --> 00:03:20.116
Each run is sorted, but not against the others, so the runs still have to be merged.

00:03:20.166 --> 00:03:23.821
Two at a time: twelve runs, then six, three, two, one.

00:03:23.871 --> 00:03:25.039
Four passes.

00:03:25.289 --> 00:03:27.473
Watch memory during a two-way merge.

00:03:27.523 --> 00:03:31.455
It holds the front block of each run, and one block for the output.

00:03:31.505 --> 00:03:33.479
The rest of memory sits idle.

00:03:33.729 --> 00:03:39.388
So give every run its own block of memory, and merge them all at once: a multiway merge.

00:03:39.638 --> 00:03:47.321
With thousands of runs, scanning every front for the smallest item would be slow, so keep a small heap with one entry per run.

00:03:47.371 --> 00:03:52.536
Unlike heapsort's heap, which was the whole file, this one sits in memory the whole time.

00:03:52.586 --> 00:03:56.787
Say run three has the smallest front item: it moves to the output.

00:03:56.837 --> 00:03:59.825
When the output fills, that block goes to disk.

00:03:59.875 --> 00:04:03.748
When run three runs dry, its next block comes in from disk.

00:04:03.998 --> 00:04:06.844
Every block is still read once and written once.

00:04:08.394 --> 00:04:15.375
So as long as memory has a block for every run, plus one for the output, the merge happens in a single pass.

00:04:15.425 --> 00:04:18.260
That folds the four merge passes into one.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:22.352
Add the pass that made the runs, and eighteen passes have become two.

00:04:22.602 --> 00:04:27.849
Sort memory-sized runs, then merge them from disk: that's external merge sort.

00:04:27.899 --> 00:04:32.734
Merging as many runs at once as memory allows keeps the passes to a minimum.

00:04:32.984 --> 00:04:35.161
It's exactly what sort did at the end.

00:04:35.211 --> 00:04:39.914
It read all twelve runs at once while the sorted file grew, and then deleted them.

00:04:42.064 --> 00:04:51.581
Our file needed only twelve runs, and GNU sort merges up to sixteen at a time by default, a cautious setting that trades some speed for less memory.

00:04:51.631 --> 00:04:53.270
That was plenty for twelve.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.700
But how many runs could one merge take?

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:58.880
One per block of memory, less one for the output.

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:10.728
Real sorting programs read much bigger blocks than our simulation did, often a megabyte, to cut down on fetches, and a laptop's memory still holds about sixteen thousand of those.

00:05:10.978 --> 00:05:16.114
A big enough file needs even more runs than that, so it takes more than one merge pass.

00:05:16.164 --> 00:05:18.770
Say a file needed a hundred thousand runs.

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:22.846
Merging sixteen thousand at a time, one pass cuts them to seven.

00:05:22.896 --> 00:05:25.004
One more pass makes them one file.

00:05:25.054 --> 00:05:30.593
So each extra merge pass lets the file grow by that same factor of sixteen thousand.

00:05:30.643 --> 00:05:34.764
In practice, even enormous files sort in two or three passes.

00:05:35.014 --> 00:05:37.182
That's why databases sort this way.

00:05:37.232 --> 00:05:46.850
Ask PostgreSQL how it ran a query that sorts more than fits in its memory, and it reports an external merge: the same runs, then merges.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:53.362
So, when data doesn't fit in memory, count I/Os, not comparisons.

00:05:53.612 --> 00:05:58.648
Work in passes that read and write in order, so every block you fetch gets used.

00:05:58.898 --> 00:06:06.172
And cut the passes: make runs as big as memory, then merge as many runs at once as memory has blocks to spare.

00:06:06.422 --> 00:06:09.604
Python tried to hold the whole file in memory at once.

00:06:09.654 --> 00:06:12.056
Sort never held more than a memory's worth.

00:06:12.306 --> 00:06:19.459
That's how sort handled a file about twelve times bigger than its memory: twelve runs, one merge, two passes.
